WEBVTT

1
00:00:07.391 --> 00:00:18.021
The next day Adele and I had to vacate the library because Mr.

2
00:00:18.102 --> 00:00:21.625
Rochester wanted to use it as a reception room for callers.

3
00:00:22.826 --> 00:00:27.871
A fire was lit in an upstairs room, and there I carried our books and arranged it as

4
00:00:27.911 --> 00:00:28.512
a schoolroom.

5
00:00:29.644 --> 00:00:35.128
Thornfield was no longer as quiet as a church; every hour or two, it echoed with a knock

6
00:00:35.168 --> 00:00:35.748
at the door.

7
00:00:36.989 --> 00:00:40.972
Footsteps crossed the hall and new voices spoke in the rooms below.

8
00:00:40.992 --> 00:00:45.595
A stream from the outer world was flowing through Thornfield.

9
00:00:46.736 --> 00:00:50.019
The house had a master, and I liked it better that way.

10
00:00:51.219 --> 00:00:53.261
Adele was not easy to teach that day.

11
00:00:54.462 --> 00:00:57.304
She kept coming up with excuses to go downstairs.

12
00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:03.582
I suspected she was trying to visit the library, where I knew she wasn't wanted.

13
00:01:04.723 --> 00:01:10.065
When I made her sit still, she continued to prattle on about her friend, Mr.

14
00:01:10.265 --> 00:01:12.046
Edward Fairfax Rochester.

15
00:01:13.947 --> 00:01:16.187
That was the first time I'd heard his full name.

16
00:01:17.348 --> 00:01:22.870
Apparently he'd hinted to Adele that there would be a gift for her when his luggage arrived from

17
00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:28.365
Millcote. "Maybe there will be one for you too," she said to me in French.

18
00:01:28.385 --> 00:01:32.409
"Last night he was asking me about my governess."

19
00:01:33.650 --> 00:01:35.832
The afternoon was wild and snowy.

20
00:01:36.973 --> 00:01:41.897
When it grew dark, and there were no more guests, I allowed Adele to run downstairs.

21
00:01:43.178 --> 00:01:47.241
Left alone, I went to a window but could see nothing outside.

22
00:01:48.522 --> 00:01:51.004
I sat by the fire and stared at the embers.

23
00:01:52.286 --> 00:01:53.647
After a while Mrs.

24
00:01:53.707 --> 00:01:56.029
Fairfax came in and said that Mr.

25
00:01:56.089 --> 00:01:59.152
Rochester would like Adele and me to take tea with him.

26
00:01:59.172 --> 00:02:01.654
"Should I change my frock?"

27
00:02:02.054 --> 00:02:02.515
I asked.

28
00:02:02.535 --> 00:02:07.619
"Yes. I always dress up for the evening when he's here.

29
00:02:08.740 --> 00:02:10.802
You'll want to wear a brooch, if you have one."

30
00:02:12.703 --> 00:02:17.688
So I put on my black silk dress and a small pearl pin that Miss Temple had given

31
00:02:17.748 --> 00:02:19.830
me. I let Mrs.

32
00:02:19.870 --> 00:02:22.152
Fairfax precede me into the dining room.

33
00:02:23.388 --> 00:02:26.571
Adele was kneeling next to Pilot in front of the fireplace.

34
00:02:27.792 --> 00:02:32.956
Mr. Rochester was watching them as he lay on a couch, his foot supported by a pillow.

35
00:02:34.197 --> 00:02:38.921
I recognized my traveler with his black hair and eyebrows and his grim mouth.

36
00:02:40.183 --> 00:02:45.747
Without his traveling cloak, his figure appeared athletic, though neither tall nor graceful.

37
00:02:46.948 --> 00:02:50.872
He must have been aware of our entrance, but he appeared not to notice us.

38
00:02:52.168 --> 00:02:54.629
"Here is Miss Eyre, sir," said Mrs.

39
00:02:54.669 --> 00:03:00.731
Fairfax. He bowed his head, still not taking his eyes from Adele and Pilot.

40
00:03:01.971 --> 00:03:03.971
"Let Miss Eyre be seated," he said.

41
00:03:05.232 --> 00:03:11.234
There was something in the stiff, forced bow and his impatient yet formal tone that made me think

42
00:03:11.254 --> 00:03:13.254
he didn't care if I were there or not.

43
00:03:15.055 --> 00:03:19.156
If he'd treated me with excessive politeness, I might have been intimidated.

44
00:03:20.332 --> 00:03:24.896
But his manner was so eccentric that I was curious to see what he would do next.

45
00:03:26.137 --> 00:03:30.561
He behaved like a statue; that is, he neither spoke nor moved.

46
00:03:31.742 --> 00:03:36.887
Mrs. Fairfax began to make some polite conversation about his visitors and his injury.

47
00:03:36.907 --> 00:03:41.711
"Mrs. Fairfax, I would like some tea" was his only response.

48
00:03:43.072 --> 00:03:46.716
She rang for tea, and when it arrived, she asked me to pour it.

49
00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:49.321
As I handed a cup to Mr.

50
00:03:49.401 --> 00:03:55.003
Rochester, Adele cried out in French, "Do you have a gift for Miss Eyre in your luggage?"

51
00:03:55.023 --> 00:03:57.484
"What's this about gifts?"

52
00:03:57.924 --> 00:03:59.645
he responded gruffly in English.

53
00:03:59.665 --> 00:04:02.446
"Did you expect a gift, Miss Eyre?

54
00:04:03.606 --> 00:04:06.328
Are you fond of gifts?"

55
00:04:06.348 --> 00:04:08.668
"I hardly know, sir," I replied.

56
00:04:08.689 --> 00:04:11.770
"I have very little experience with them.

57
00:04:13.610 --> 00:04:17.032
But since I'm a stranger to you, I've done nothing to deserve a gift."

58
00:04:18.580 --> 00:04:19.941
"Oh, don't be so modest!"

59
00:04:20.361 --> 00:04:21.181
he scolded me.

60
00:04:21.201 --> 00:04:25.422
"I've quizzed Adele and find you've taken great pains with her.

61
00:04:26.603 --> 00:04:28.683
She's not bright and she has no talents.

62
00:04:29.904 --> 00:04:32.724
Yet in a short time, she has made great improvement."

63
00:04:32.744 --> 00:04:36.165
"Sir, you've now given me my gift.

64
00:04:37.326 --> 00:04:38.306
I'm obliged to you.

65
00:04:39.486 --> 00:04:42.867
The best gift for a teacher is praise for her student's progress."

66
00:04:44.976 --> 00:04:45.517
"Humph!" said Mr.

67
00:04:45.597 --> 00:04:48.459
Rochester, and he drank his tea in silence.

68
00:04:49.740 --> 00:04:52.903
When he had finished, he told me to come sit by the fire.

69
00:04:54.084 --> 00:04:59.029
Adele wanted to take a seat on my knee, but he ordered her to amuse herself with Pilot.

70
00:05:00.230 --> 00:05:02.792
He asked about my family and my life at Lowood.

71
00:05:02.812 --> 00:05:05.574
"I suppose you play the piano."

72
00:05:05.594 --> 00:05:06.996
"A little."

73
00:05:08.817 --> 00:05:09.298
"Of course.

74
00:05:10.539 --> 00:05:11.720
That's the expected answer.

75
00:05:12.832 --> 00:05:17.655
Go into the sitting room—I mean, please go into the sitting room and play a tune.

76
00:05:18.856 --> 00:05:20.817
I'm used to saying 'Do this,' and it's done.

77
00:05:22.057 --> 00:05:24.219
I can't change my habits for one new person."

78
00:05:25.539 --> 00:05:28.141
I went to the adjoining room to obey his instructions.

79
00:05:28.161 --> 00:05:31.643
"Enough!" he called out in a few minutes.

80
00:05:31.663 --> 00:05:37.446
"You play like any other English schoolgirl, perhaps better than some but not well."

81
00:05:38.727 --> 00:05:39.988
I returned to the dining room.

82
00:05:41.108 --> 00:05:42.749
"Adele showed me some of your sketches.

83
00:05:44.109 --> 00:05:45.749
Surely a drawing teacher helped you?"

84
00:05:46.910 --> 00:05:47.810
"No, indeed!"

85
00:05:48.230 --> 00:05:49.270
I said indignantly.

86
00:05:49.290 --> 00:05:51.991
"Ah, I've wounded your pride.

87
00:05:53.051 --> 00:05:56.552
Bring them here, and I'll determine whether they were done by one hand or two."

88
00:05:58.453 --> 00:06:03.394
I fetched my portfolio, and he went over my sketches and watercolors, which he seemed to like.

89
00:06:04.554 --> 00:06:07.135
He quizzed me: Was I happy while drawing?

90
00:06:08.235 --> 00:06:09.816
Was I satisfied with the results?

91
00:06:11.080 --> 00:06:14.342
Then he ordered me to take the pictures away and put Adele to bed.

92
00:06:15.643 --> 00:06:17.605
After I did that, I rejoined Mrs.

93
00:06:17.665 --> 00:06:18.786
Fairfax in her room.

94
00:06:18.806 --> 00:06:24.770
"Mr. Rochester is very abrupt and inconsistent," I observed.

95
00:06:25.890 --> 00:06:29.133
"No doubt he appears so to a stranger, but I'm used to him.

96
00:06:30.374 --> 00:06:32.375
We should make allowances for his temperament."

97
00:06:32.395 --> 00:06:39.220
"Why?" "Partly because it's his nature, and none of us can help our nature."

98
00:06:40.412 --> 00:06:42.234
Mrs. Fairfax picked up her knitting.

99
00:06:42.254 --> 00:06:47.178
"And partly because he has painful thoughts that harass him."

100
00:06:47.198 --> 00:06:49.060
"What kind of thoughts?"

101
00:06:50.361 --> 00:06:52.203
"Family troubles, for one thing."

102
00:06:53.384 --> 00:06:54.044
I was puzzled.

103
00:06:54.064 --> 00:06:57.187
"But he has no family."

104
00:06:58.989 --> 00:07:00.871
Mrs. Fairfax explained that Mr.

105
00:07:00.951 --> 00:07:07.840
Rochester's father had not wanted to divide his estate between Edward and his older brother, Rowland.

106
00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:12.445
But he also didn't want Edward to be impoverished when Rowland inherited everything.

107
00:07:12.465 --> 00:07:14.327
"Old Mr.

108
00:07:14.367 --> 00:07:21.254
Rochester and Rowland schemed to bring Edward into what Edward considered a painful situation for the sake of

109
00:07:21.334 --> 00:07:22.476
making his fortune.

110
00:07:23.737 --> 00:07:29.343
As a result, Edward broke with his family and shuns Thornfield," said Mrs.

111
00:07:29.403 --> 00:07:38.512
Fairfax. "After Rowland died nine years ago, Edward inherited Thornfield, but he seldom stays more than two weeks

112
00:07:38.592 --> 00:07:39.172
at a time."

113
00:07:40.433 --> 00:07:44.975
I didn't understand why my employer disliked Thornfield, but Mrs.

114
00:07:45.016 --> 00:07:49.238
Fairfax either could not or would not give me any more information.

115
00:07:50.518 --> 00:07:54.080
It was evident that she wanted me to drop the subject, and so I did.
